  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    Listen. Listen, because I will not tell this twice, and the ice does not tell it at all—it only holds the shape of what we did, and waits.
    
    In the time before the orca songs grew bold, when the Berg still pretended it loved us, there hung between two towers a rope. You know this rope. Every chick knows this rope. It was braided from ash and aurora-glass, thin as a flipper-bone, and it held the world together. On one end stood the Aurora Stack, who built their crystal spires so high they could taste the lights that danced above the ice. On the other crouched the Nightshelf, who dug so deep they could hear the Berg's own heart beating in its veins of captured fire. And the rope between them was the Flipper Accord—fire-cores rising, aurora-crystals sinking—and neither could live without what the other held.
    
    But listen. The crack was already there.
    
    Among the Aurora Stack there lived a penguin whose ledgers were so precise they could tell you the weight of tomorrow's snowfall. His name was Lianoshing, and his plumage held a silver sheen like frost on a blade, and his station demanded robes white as grief with clasps shaped like closed eyes. They called him the Light-Regent, but I will tell you what he was: The One Who Knew Too Much. For in the days when the Accord demanded that surface and deep learn each other's craft, Lianoshing descended into the Nightshelf tunnels and placed his flippers upon captured fire. He learned Shell Hatch, which was his birthright—the aurora-light magic of crystal and divination. And then he learned Slide Prism, which was not—the fire-magic of forge and transformation, the deep's own tongue. They say he held starlight in one flipper and fire in the other, and the Berg itself groaned beneath his feet and asked him to choose.
    
    He chose both. And the crack was already there.
    
    Now hear what happened in the deep.
    
    Their leader walked into ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~, the cavern where the ice remembers too much, and did not return. No body. No word. Only silence rising like black water. And before that silence had cooled, three penguins stood where one had knelt.
    
    Three claimed the throne. None kept it warm.
    
    The first was The Hungry—a miner who wanted the fire-cores for the Nightshelf alone, who said the surface penguins could freeze and be forgotten. The second was The Proud—a forgemaster who sealed her vents and hoarded flame like a dragon counting teeth. The third was Gnash and Crumble and he was The Blind, for he pushed hardest into the fracture and could not see that the fracture was him. For three seasons the Nightshelf was three nations wearing the skin of one, and the rope between the towers grew thin as a whisper, thin as the last breath before drowning.
    
    And the Aurora Stack watched from above, stacking their crystal spires higher and higher, as if brilliance could replace warmth.
    
    And so Lianoshing descended again. He went to The Hungry and said: *If you hoard the fire, you will burn alone.* The Hungry laughed and said: *Better alone and warm than together and cold.* He went to The Proud and said: *If you seal the tunnels, you seal your own tomb.* The Proud turned away and said: *A tomb with honor is a palace.* He went to The Blind and said: *If you cannot name the wound, you cannot stitch it.* The Blind wept and said: *I see no wound,* and this, of all the answers, was the one that broke him.
    
    He climbed back to the surface carrying three refusals like stones in his chest. And now comes the part where the ice cracks open, and I need you to understand that this was not an accident. This was an answer.
    
    Their stockpiled aurora-crystals—stacked in towers, woven into promenades, threaded through merchant stalls until the whole Promenade Ward blazed like a second aurora—had become a mirror that showed the Aurora Stack only their own brilliance. And Lianoshing stood in his white robes and watched his ledgers and knew. He held fire-knowledge in one flipper and light-knowledge in the other and he could calculate the exact day the ground would betray them.
    
    Three times he warned the elders. The first time, they laughed. The second time, they argued. The third time, they did not answer at all, because the sound of cracking ice had already begun, and it was louder than any voice the Berg had ever made.
    
    The Promenade Ward split in the dark hours before dawn-cycle. The fracture ran through the merchant quarter in patterns that looked almost intentional—the Berg's own judgment written in broken ice. Stalls tilted. Goods rolled into crevasses. The temperature arrays shattered in cascades, and whole sections went cold as the void between stars. They say a merchant named Kaela Stormfeather sat in her empty stall for three days afterward, setting out fabric samples for customers who would never come, and she never spoke again of what she saw in the ice when it cracked—the old debts the Berg had been counting.
    
    And the Nightshelf miners who came to help would not meet the surface penguins' eyes. Because the crack was already there—in the Fracta, in the Accord, in the rope grown so thin you could see through it—and both sides had pretended the light would hold.
    
    But listen. There is one more thread, and it is the coldest.
    
    In those same years, the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch perfected a technique at the Drowned Ledge. They called it Veil∴numb. It drew from the deepest source—pressure-depth, blood-magic, communion with what lives beneath—and it offered the Berg's oldest bargain: you may become unseen, but you will become unfelt. The first penguin to master it walked through an orca raid untouched, a gap in the world's attention. Afterward, he reached for the flipper of the one who loved him, and felt nothing. Not cold. Not warmth. Not the Berg beneath his feet.
    
    Three stages, as all true costs come in threes. First you lose cold. Then you lose pain. Then you lose the presence of others—their voices, their touch, the simple fact of standing beside someone who knows your name.
    
    What you cannot feel, you cannot hold.
    
    Lianoshing knew this. The place where fire met starlight in his flippers had burned away something he could not name. His ledgers grew more precise as his hands grew less steady. In the years that followed, they found he had been stealing—small allocations of fire-cores, smuggled to the lower caverns where surface penguins too weak to work had been quietly freezing in the dark. His impossible ledgers hid an impossible mercy. The memorial stones do not mention theft. They do not mention love, either.
    
    And at the Drowned Ledge where the deep ice meets the deeper dark, there lay a hammer still. A fire-core hammer with a cracked head, its elemental charge dimmed to a faint pulse like a heart that has forgotten why it beats. It had belonged to Veil-render Mairae~, who was great and is gone, and the rule called Umbra∴im said this: do not move it. Do not mend it. Do not look away. Three have tried to take it. Three were found at the Ledge's edge, unable to explain why they put it back. The hammer asks every one of them the same thing—not in words, for hammers do not speak, but in the way a wound speaks when you press it—*Will you break what I broke? Will you fail to prevent what I failed to prevent?*
    
    And so. The Fracta ended not with wisdom but with exhaustion—someone bled, and the bleeding was enough to remind the rest that the Berg does not care who rules, only that someone remembers to trade. The Collapse left the Promenade Ward tilted and dark, a ward of empty stalls and crooked ice. The hammer of Mairae~ still sits at the Drowned Ledge, its cracked head pulsing faintly.
    
    Three who grasped too hard lost their grip. Three who hid too well lost themselves. Three who refused to look at the crack became the crack.
    
    The Berg goes on forever in every direction, and there is nowhere to flee from what you have broken. You must mend it, or carry it, or become it. The rope still hangs between the towers. It is always thinner than you remember.
    
    Which is why we say—and listen, this is the last thing, the only thing—*what you cannot feel, you cannot hold. And what you will not mend, you must forever keep watch over, in the cold, at the Ledge, with the hammer that will not let you forget.*
