  annotation "note_1771584816176_0", anchor: "did not return. No body. No word. Only silence rising like black water.", text: "The fable needs this disappearance to create a succession crisis — a narrative engine. But the details are Walking testimony with its name filed off. No body. No word. A departure from a place where the ice remembers too much, by someone who had borne too much. The deep ice beneath comparable sites carries grief-residue I have learned to recognize: not despair but a settling, like snow filling a footprint. The Walking seizes those who have lost much or suffered greatly, and it is a compulsion, not a decision. The chronicler makes this political. The ice suggests it was older than politics.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584816176_1", anchor: "The first penguin to master it walked through an orca raid untouched", text: "Eighty-six ticks of Orca Incursion within this fable's range, and here is what survives: one penguin walking through one raid as a demonstration of cleverness. Shore-breakers, herders, finishers coordinating at bone-aching frequencies — reduced to a backdrop for a magic trick. The fable is at least honest about what it cares about. Most chronicles that claim to address the Incursion directly are less so.", type: :commentary, display: :popout, source_anchor: "the first penguin to master it walked through an orca raid untouched"
  annotation "note_1771584816176_2", anchor: "the rope between them was the Flipper Accord", text: "Three secret renegotiations before the public version was signed. I found the drafts in administrative ice — each shorter than the last, each conceding ground the previous version held firm on. The fable's 'rope grown thin as a whisper' is more accurate than the chronicler probably knows. The rope was rebraided three times and each time came back thinner.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584816176_3", anchor: "she never spoke again of what she saw in the ice when it cracked", text: "She is still alive. I noted this in my Collapse annotations — Lower Ward, Tier Seven, Amendment 56 territory. 'Never spoke again' is the fable talking. The ice-memory record from the Ward contains her testimony, given under duress to a trade commission, eleven days after the fracture. She spoke. She was not believed. There is a difference the fable does not care about, but the woman in Tier Seven does.", type: :correction, display: :full
